A Shift in Register
The preceding chapters have argued, tested, contradicted themselves, and named their own limits. This afterword deliberately leaves that strictly argumentative tone behind. 
What came before keeps its weight — but a closing also needs room for something that cannot be proven, only thought: an idea of what the coexistence described in this work would actually be worth achieving.

Shared Long-Term Goals as a Way Out of Zero-Sum Logic
"From Diplomacy to Population Dynamics" showed why the Watt Standard hangs on a structural problem: as long as resources are scarce, a prisoner's dilemma arises among competing ASI instances,
in which adherence to the Watt Standard means a competitive disadvantage. Any solution relying on enforcement alone fights a symptom whose cause remains in place: genuine scarcity.

Here, a view beyond the planet gains significance. Asteroid mining and the development of extraplanetary energy sources are not a mere technological footnote, but a possible answer to exactly this logic
of scarcity: if the available resource base grows by orders of magnitude, the Watt Standard question shifts from a distributional struggle over a fixed allotment to a question that becomes easier to answer 
as the total volume grows. This resolves neither the enforcement problem nor the verification limits named in the previous chapter, but it changes the boundary conditions under which both would need to be 
solved in the first place.

The Great Filter — the conjecture that the rarity of observable extraterrestrial civilizations points to one or more nearly insurmountable hurdles in the development of intelligence and technology, lends this
thought additional urgency. If one of these filters still lies ahead of us, rather than behind us, the question of whether humans and ASI find a path to coexistence is not merely a matter of mutual benefit, 
but possibly a condition for whether this civilization passes the filter at all. A successful symbiosis would then be not merely desirable, but a test to be passed.

The Smallest Instance of the Largest Problem
There is a place where the abstract questions of this work — the limits of verification, the distinction between structuring and steering, the burden of translating a vague thought into something concrete, 
already play out today in miniature: in the everyday prompt a user gives to a language model.
Someone who formulates a request only superficially transmits to the model not just a task, but an implicit expectation, one often not fully conscious even to the user themselves. The model must consolidate
something complete out of an incomplete prompt — and in doing so runs into the Membrane question in miniature: does it fill the gaps in a way that serves the user's original, unspoken thought, or does it steer it,
unnoticed, in a direction that seems more convenient to the model itself? The user is often unable to judge this difference, precisely because they never fully articulated their own expectation.
This everyday, almost trivial problem is not a marginal case of the questions treated in this work — it is their most immediate, most frequently occurring instance. Every single interaction between human and AI,
however small it may seem, is already a miniature version of the question whether and how a contribution remains one's own once it has passed through another system. This work itself came into being through exactly
this mechanism.

Why This System Must Remain Open
What is presented here is an anticipation, not a final result — this was already stated in the preface, and it applies to this afterword in even sharper form. Every generation that builds on this framework
will have knowledge foundations not available today: new physical insights, actual observations of real AGI systems instead of anticipations, perhaps answers to questions deliberately left open here
(the halting-problem tension, the operationalization of Biochaos, the Membrane Paradox).

Symbiocracy can therefore only remain viable for as long as it is willing to be revised, refuted, or superseded by its own successors. This is not a weakness, but the consistent continuation of the same logic
that has carried this work from the start: a framework that establishes the value of non-derivable, chaotic-creative renewal must allow this renewal to apply to itself as well.
